{"name":"Liquid Hydrogen","hex":"#cedfe0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"liquid","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A substance that is flowing, and keeping no shape, such as water; a substance of which the molecules, while not tending to separate from one another like those of a gas, readily change their relative position, and which therefore retains no definite shape, except that determined by the containing receptacle; an inelastic fluid.","Any of a class of consonant sounds that includes l and r."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Flowing freely like water; fluid; not solid and not gaseous; composed of particles that move freely among each other on the slightest pressure.","Easily sold or disposed of without losing value."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/liquid"},{"term":"hydrogen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The lightest chemical element (symbol H), with an atomic number of 1 and atomic weight of 1.008.","An atom of this element."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hydrogen"}],"entries":[{"title":"Liquid hydrogen","blocks":[{"text":"Liquid hydrogen (H2(l)) is the liquid state of the element hydrogen. Hydrogen is found naturally in the molecular H2 form."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Liquid_hydrogen_bubblechamber.jpg/330px-Liquid_hydrogen_bubblechamber.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liquid_hydrogen","wikidata":"Q622779"},{"title":"Liquid","blocks":[{"text":"Liquid is a state of matter with a definite volume but no fixed shape. When confined in a container and subject to a force such as gravity, liquids will adapt to the internal shape of the container in the direction of the force. Liquids are nearly incompressible, maintaining their volume even under pressure. The density of a liquid is usually close to that of a solid, and much higher than that of a gas. Liquids are a form of condensed matter alongside solids, and a form of fluid alongside gases."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Water_drop_001.jpg/330px-Water_drop_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liquid","wikidata":"Q11435"},{"title":"Hydrogen","blocks":[{"text":"Hydrogen is a chemical element; it has the symbol H and atomic number 1. It is the lightest and most abundant chemical element in the universe, constituting about 75% of all normal matter. Under standard conditions, hydrogen is a gas of diatomic molecules with the formula H2, called dihydrogen, or sometimes hydrogen gas, molecular hydrogen, or simply hydrogen. Dihydrogen is colorless, odorless, non-toxic, and highly combustible. Stars, including the Sun, mainly consist of hydrogen in a plasma state, while on Earth, hydrogen is found as the gas H2 (dihydrogen) and in molecules, such as in water and organic compounds. The most common isotope of hydrogen, 1H, consists of one proton, one electron, and no neutrons."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Hydrogen_discharge_tube.jpg/330px-Hydrogen_discharge_tube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrogen","wikidata":"Q556"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Dihydrogen-2D-dimensions.png/500px-Dihydrogen-2D-dimensions.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Dihydrogen-2D-dimensions.png/500px-Dihydrogen-2D-dimensions.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADihydrogen-2D-dimensions.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Dihydrogen-3D-vdW.png/500px-Dihydrogen-3D-vdW.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Dihydrogen-3D-vdW.png/500px-Dihydrogen-3D-vdW.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADihydrogen-3D-vdW.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Hydrogen_Tank_-_GPN-2000-001458.jpg/500px-Hydrogen_Tank_-_GPN-2000-001458.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Hydrogen_Tank_-_GPN-2000-001458.jpg/1280px-Hydrogen_Tank_-_GPN-2000-001458.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Hydrogen_Tank_-_GPN-2000-001458.jpg/500px-Hydrogen_Tank_-_GPN-2000-001458.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A large hydrogen tank in a vacuum chamber at the Glenn Research Center in Brook Park, Ohio, in 1967","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHydrogen_Tank_-_GPN-2000-001458.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Linde-Wasserstofftank.JPG/500px-Linde-Wasserstofftank.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Linde-Wasserstofftank.JPG/1280px-Linde-Wasserstofftank.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Linde-Wasserstofftank.JPG/500px-Linde-Wasserstofftank.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Linde AG tank for liquid hydrogen at the Museum Autovision in Altlußheim, Germany, in 2008","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALinde-Wasserstofftank.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/DOT_Hazardous_Mat